1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,392
One platform, three applications.

2
00:00:02,392 --> 00:00:08,117
Recover, the first, decides the best next life for every asset that comes back.

3
00:00:08,117 --> 00:00:21,525
Resolve brings signals, alternatives, constraints and dependencies into one decision flow, prioritises what matters, coordinates actions across teams and systems, and tracks resolution.

4
00:00:21,525 --> 00:00:35,078
Reconcile validates commercial and operational evidence across records, identifies mismatches early, and generates evidence-backed proformas before invoices are issued and disputes arise.

5
00:00:35,078 --> 00:00:36,890
Let's start with Recover.

6
00:00:36,870 --> 00:00:41,030
We'll sign in as the planner who looks after returns at this site.

7
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:47,599
This cycle, sixty-four assets came back for this customer.

8
00:00:47,599 --> 00:00:51,720
Our agent has already sorted them, and marked where to start.

9
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:56,307
Take this one, a fifty-five kilowatt motor back from a lease.

10
00:00:56,307 --> 00:01:02,850
Our agent pulled its owner, grade, certificate and history from the systems that hold them.

11
00:01:02,850 --> 00:01:04,720
Nobody keyed any of it in.

12
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:07,893
Ask the agent, and it sums the motor up:

13
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:13,500
grade B, all ten pieces of evidence in, and its recommendation, redeploy.

14
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:18,587
Its journey has ten stages.

15
00:01:18,587 --> 00:01:21,620
Our agent ran the first five on its own.

16
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:23,395
Stage one.

17
00:01:23,395 --> 00:01:24,571
The lease ended.

18
00:01:24,571 --> 00:01:28,100
Our agent read the contract and opened the case.

19
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:29,924
Stage two.

20
00:01:29,924 --> 00:01:37,290
Order management authorised the return, and our agent found a customer order that needs this motor.

21
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:39,177
Stage three.

22
00:01:39,177 --> 00:01:43,740
The carrier booked the pickup, and our agent tracked it in transit.

23
00:01:44,830 --> 00:01:45,578
Stage four.

24
00:01:45,578 --> 00:01:51,020
The warehouse received it, and our agent checked that the evidence was complete.

25
00:01:52,090 --> 00:01:52,911
Stage five.

26
00:01:52,911 --> 00:01:55,972
The MRO workshop inspected and graded it.

27
00:01:55,972 --> 00:02:00,004
Ask the agent about this stage, and it shows its work:

28
00:02:00,004 --> 00:02:07,620
the grade from the workshop system, the certificate from quality, and why that leaves six routes open.

29
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:11,588
Stage six is the decision.

30
00:02:11,588 --> 00:02:19,450
Our agent built seven possible routes, checked each one for eligibility, and ruled one out before pricing.

31
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:24,060
Then it predicted each outcome with its machine-learning models.

32
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:25,369
The recommendation:

33
00:02:25,369 --> 00:02:28,883
redeploy against this customer's replacement order.

34
00:02:28,883 --> 00:02:34,050
Ninety-six hundred dollars net, forty-eight hundred better than selling it.

35
00:02:36,170 --> 00:02:38,679
Ask it why, and the agent explains.

36
00:02:38,679 --> 00:02:46,852
It saves buying a new motor for that order, the refurbishment is modest, and the certificate covers the full term.

37
00:02:46,852 --> 00:02:49,361
On the right, the evidence it used:

38
00:02:49,361 --> 00:02:52,730
each figure, the system it came from, and when.

39
00:02:52,930 --> 00:02:53,942
Now stress it.

40
00:02:53,942 --> 00:02:56,833
Put the workshop cost up thirty percent:

41
00:02:56,833 --> 00:02:59,508
the agent still ranks redeploy first.

42
00:02:59,508 --> 00:03:03,266
Cancel the customer's order, and the agent re-ranks.

43
00:03:03,266 --> 00:03:07,820
Selling becomes the better route, and it tells you by how much.

44
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:11,917
You see the agent's reasoning, not just its answer.

45
00:03:11,917 --> 00:03:14,720
The planner is satisfied, and submits it.

46
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:19,758
Stage seven is where a second person decides.

47
00:03:19,758 --> 00:03:22,118
Our agent drafted the rationale.

48
00:03:22,118 --> 00:03:26,027
The approver reads it, checks their limit, and signs.

49
00:03:26,027 --> 00:03:29,050
Nothing goes out until a person approves.

50
00:03:30,690 --> 00:03:32,318
Stages eight and nine.

51
00:03:32,318 --> 00:03:36,093
Our agent sends the work to the systems that do it:

52
00:03:36,093 --> 00:03:42,309
the customer order, the MRO refurbishment work order, the warehouse and the carrier.

53
00:03:42,309 --> 00:03:46,750
Each one reports back, and the agent tracks every reference.

54
00:03:46,970 --> 00:03:47,705
Stage ten.

55
00:03:47,705 --> 00:03:49,618
Our agent closes the loop:

56
00:03:49,618 --> 00:03:53,809
predicted, ninety-six hundred; actual, ninety-two thirty.

57
00:03:53,809 --> 00:03:55,501
Ask it what it learned.

58
00:03:55,501 --> 00:04:06,900
The biggest difference was in the workshop, a shaft sleeve found worn at strip-down, and it feeds that line back to the model that estimates workshop cost.

59
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:16,621
And this is how our agents ran all ten stages, replayed at twice the speed.

60
00:04:16,621 --> 00:04:19,266
Five agents, each with a narrow job:

61
00:04:19,266 --> 00:04:27,055
gathering evidence, matching demand, preparing the decision, sending the work out, reconciling the result.

62
00:04:27,055 --> 00:04:29,701
Every tool call is named and logged.

63
00:04:29,701 --> 00:04:32,640
People made the two calls that mattered.

64
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:38,422
All of this runs on top of the systems you already have:

65
00:04:38,422 --> 00:04:43,598
your ERP, order management, the MRO workshop, warehouse and quality.

66
00:04:43,598 --> 00:04:46,794
We don't replace them; we connect to them.

67
00:04:46,794 --> 00:04:49,078
This is the Connection Fabric:

68
00:04:49,078 --> 00:04:51,970
twenty systems, connected as they are.

69
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:57,332
The source map shows how data flows between them.

70
00:04:57,332 --> 00:05:02,627
Pick the MRO workshop, and our agent lights up where its data goes:

71
00:05:02,627 --> 00:05:06,342
the condition grading and workshop cost models.

72
00:05:06,342 --> 00:05:10,530
Pick the ERP, and you see the demand and price paths.

73
00:05:10,630 --> 00:05:16,435
Every system reaches one of seven shared models, and from there, the decision.

74
00:05:16,435 --> 00:05:19,933
When a feed goes stale, our agent diagnoses it.

75
00:05:19,933 --> 00:05:24,920
Here, an expired token on the market price feed, and how to fix it.

76
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:27,987
Source tables.

77
00:05:27,987 --> 00:05:33,928
Our agent read each system's own tables, as they are, and proposed how they link:

78
00:05:33,928 --> 00:05:37,009
twelve links here, each with its evidence.

79
00:05:37,009 --> 00:05:40,530
Your team accepts them, or changes them by hand.

80
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:43,715
Canonical models.

81
00:05:43,715 --> 00:05:49,490
One shared language for an asset, its condition, the demand and the decision.

82
00:05:49,490 --> 00:05:57,590
Our agent maps every system's fields into it, so a grade in one system means the same as a grade in another.

83
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:00,253
Workflow Studio.

84
00:06:00,253 --> 00:06:05,112
The pipelines that bring data in and send approved instructions out.

85
00:06:05,112 --> 00:06:10,400
Our agent set these up, and your team approved them before they went live.

86
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:11,509
Need a new one?

87
00:06:11,509 --> 00:06:12,788
Ask in plain words.

88
00:06:12,788 --> 00:06:17,430
Our agent drafts the steps, checks them, and builds it on the canvas.

89
00:06:20,890 --> 00:06:22,504
Change it the same way.

90
00:06:22,504 --> 00:06:26,996
Every five minutes, and flag any unit that fails its bench test.

91
00:06:26,996 --> 00:06:30,505
The agent shows the change first, then applies it.

92
00:06:30,505 --> 00:06:32,470
A person still publishes it.

93
00:06:34,210 --> 00:06:36,263
And how do you know it works?

94
00:06:36,263 --> 00:06:40,157
Our agent builds this chart from every closed decision.

95
00:06:40,157 --> 00:06:41,290
Each dot is one:

96
00:06:41,290 --> 00:06:44,830
what it predicted, against what actually happened.

97
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:51,796
With each model version, the error comes down, from about eighteen percent to twelve and a half.

98
00:06:51,796 --> 00:06:56,370
Ask the agent what changed, and it shows you, version by version.

99
00:06:58,040 --> 00:06:59,103
So, to sum up.

100
00:06:59,103 --> 00:07:03,050
It works wherever assets come back, in any industry.

101
00:07:03,050 --> 00:07:12,160
It sits on top of your ERP, your order management and your MRO workshop, and makes better use of what they already know.

102
00:07:12,570 --> 00:07:14,510
The agents do the groundwork:

103
00:07:14,510 --> 00:07:17,253
the evidence, the options, the paperwork.

104
00:07:17,253 --> 00:07:19,327
Your people make the decisions.

105
00:07:19,327 --> 00:07:22,940
And every decision gets a little better than the last.
